#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_monotonicity

#% text
# `function_monotonicity(f, x)` gives `1` when `f` is nondecreasing on its domain, `-1` when nonincreasing, `0` when constant, and `indeterminate` otherwise.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# It is the sign of the derivative `d(f, x)` on the domain of `f` (see `function_sign`).

#% item
# `function_monotonicity([f, cond], x)` restricts the domain; `function_monotonicity(f, n, integers)` uses differences for a sequence.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The exponential and the sine:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(e^x, x)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
function_monotonicity(sin(x), x)
#% output
#= indeterminate

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The logarithm on its domain:

#% cell
function_monotonicity([log(x), x > 0], x)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# A function constant on half the line:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(sqrt(x^2) - x, x)
#% output
#= -1

#% text
# A sequence:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(n^2, n, integers)
#% output
#= indeterminate

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A strictly monotonic function is injective:

#% cell
function_injective(x^3 + x, x)
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `function_sign`, `function_convexity`, `function_injective`, `d`
